  "query_preview": "..\n\nTonight into Saturday: A deep upper trough has developed over the\nRockies that is expected to form into a closed low over south-\ncentral Canada Saturday. A cold front, is pushing east into NE\nMontana this afternoon. Strong NW winds behind it will be in place\ntonight across the area, slowly diminishing Saturday. High winds\n(gusts to 58 mph and/or sustained at 40 mph for 2 hours) are not\nexpected. The winds according to the HREF and NBM should weaken a\nbit from what is observed upstream.\n\nA band of rainshowers over central Montana are also pushing NE \ninto the western zones. These will continue NE but the greatest \nQPF is expected over Phillips county with a Storm Total near \n0.25-0.50\" but locally higher in the Little Rockies where some \nsnow is expected. Models are shaky on how much will fall but \nZortman stands to get about an inch or two this evening.\n\nSurface low over E MT this afternoon will move into W Manitoba\ntonight. NW winds are expected to decrease Saturday as the PGF\ndecreases. Lake Wind Advisory till noon looks good.\n\nBut winds switch around to the SE for Sunday and Monday and\nanother LWA may be needed.\n\nSunday and Next Week: An upper trough will remain over the area \nwith some decent cold Canadian air being feed in at low levels \ninto next week. This will keep temps below normal. A weak system\ncould bring a some light rain or snow but total accumulations \nlook to be less than a tenth of an inch. Possible storm later in\nthe week may mean more business.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nNO changes to NBM. TFJ",
